#{[17592186278373 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=(x-2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=(x-2)^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2(x-2).$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2(x-2)^{3},\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278375 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278376 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={e}^{x}(x-1)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [2,3].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+(x{e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278377 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=z\\sqrt{z}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278378 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278391 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278425 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u-v,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =xy+z\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=0} \\\\  & =(u+v)(u-v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278427 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =2xz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278442 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u,\\,-3v,\\,4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-3,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2uv\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278443 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u-2v,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278450 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,uv,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3,\\,uv,\\,v⟩\\cdot ⟨-v,\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-v)+uv\\cdot (-2{u}^{2})+v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-3uv-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4}v+{u}^{2}v]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[-3{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278455 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2v \\\\ 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u+2v,\\,2u+2v,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-2uv} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\,0,\\,v-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u-v,\\,0,\\,v-u⟩\\cdot ⟨2u+2v,\\,2u+2v,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (2u+2v)+0\\cdot (2u+2v)+(v-u)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}-u{v}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}v-\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278459 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨-(u+v)-(-v),\\,u+v-(v-u),\\,-(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u,\\,2u,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-u,\\,2u,\\,v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-u)\\cdot 1+2u\\cdot 1+v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-u+2u+2v \\\\  & =u+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(u+2v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u+2v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[uv+{v}^{2}]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(u+1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+u]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278461 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v\\, \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278462 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\,{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278463 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,0,\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\,0,\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278464 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\,{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278470 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}-3,\\,2-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩{|}_{x=u+v\\,y={u}^{2}+2v\\,z={u}^{3}+3v} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}-3,\\,2-2u⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (6u-6{u}^{2})+0\\cdot (3{u}^{2}-3)+2\\cdot (2-2u) \\\\  & =6u-6{u}^{2}+4-4u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{2}+2u+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6)\\cdot {u}^{2}+2\\cdot u+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+2+4=0.\n$$"] [17592186278471 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{-v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{v},\\,{e}^{-v},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{-v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v},\\,-u{e}^{-v},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{-v},\\,u{e}^{v},\\,-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\,y+\\frac{1}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u{e}^{v}\\,y=u{e}^{-v}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\,u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\,u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\,u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨u{e}^{-v},\\,u{e}^{v},\\,-2u⟩ \\\\  & =(u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{-v}+(u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{v}+u\\cdot (-2u) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{e}^{-v}+{u}^{2}+{e}^{v}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}-{e}^{-v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot {e}^{v}+(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1-1=0.\n$$"] [17592186278495 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-3\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-2v)\\cdot (-3)+(-v)\\cdot (2)+2u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+6v-2v+2u \\\\  & =-7u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278510 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-(u-2v),\\,u+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(3v-u)\\cdot 2+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u+6v-2u+u+v \\\\  & =-2u+7v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278512 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278515 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=-{x}^{2}z,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,-{x}^{2}z,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,-2xz,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2xz & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{x}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-2xz,\\,-1,\\,-{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2P\\,xz+Q+R\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278516 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=2yz,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨2yz,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨2z,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨2y,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2z & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2y & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(y,z))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(\\,-2Q)\\,z+(\\,-2R)\\,y\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278518 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,v+2(v-u),\\,2u-v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(v-u)\\cdot 1+(3v-2u)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u-3v+2u+2u-v \\\\  & =3u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278519 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P-Q\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281348 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v{e}^{u} & {e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{v} & u{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v{e}^{u}\\cdot u{e}^{v}-{e}^{v}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =uv{e}^{u+v}-{e}^{u+v} \\\\  & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,1)} & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v}\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=1} \\\\  & =(-1\\cdot 1-1){e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-2|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =2\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281366 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281367 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281368 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281371 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =\\left(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v}\\right)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281372 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281409 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [17592186281410 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9-6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [17592186281411 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [17592186281412 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a triangle."] [17592186281413 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."] [17592186281463 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The  matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $S.$” is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$” is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with real entries,   there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice if $A\\in S$ then \n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot |A|=|A|\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement “For any $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have $A+(B-C)=(A+B)-C.$” is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in S,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+(B-C)=A+(B+(-C))=(A+B)+(-C)=(A+B)-C\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$”; “For any $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have $A+(B-C)=(A+B)-C.$” only."] [17592186284418 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=2$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is positive-definite” is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is positive-definite”; “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” only."] [17592186284419 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is negative-definite” only."] [17592186284423 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite” is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite”; “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$” only."] [17592186284424 :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-19$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $det(A)=-19$; “$A$ is negative-definite” only."] [17592186287820 :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2p-4+10)-0+(-10-6) \\\\  & =2p-4+10-10-6 \\\\  & =2p-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-10 & =0 \\\\ 2p & =10 \\\\ p & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=5.$"] [17592186287821 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{vmatrix} & =4\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ y & -5\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}-3y & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-3y & -2 \\\\ 2 & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (10-y)-2\\cdot (15y-2)+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =40-4y-30y+4-9{y}^{2}+12 \\\\  & =-9{y}^{2}-34y+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $81,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{y}^{2}-34y+56 & =81 \\\\ -9{y}^{2}-34y-25 & =0 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+34y+25 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{{34}^{2}-4\\cdot 9\\cdot 25}}{2\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{256}}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm 16}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-17\\pm 8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $y=-1.$"] [17592186287822 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-1)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3)-3(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =39-6+8 \\\\  & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|41|=41.\n$$"] [17592186287823 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & -{k}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-{k}^{2})\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(2\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 8)-{k}^{2}(2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =18-2{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 18-2{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =9 \\\\ k & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-3$ or $k=3.$"] [17592186287824 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2k & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k\\cdot 3+0-3\\cdot (-6k) \\\\  & =-3k+18k \\\\  & =15k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-30,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15k & =-30 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287825 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}p & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ p-3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & p \\\\ p-3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (3p-{p}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3-2{p}^{2}+6p \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+6p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-23,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{p}^{2}+6p-3 & =-23 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}-6p-20 & =0 \\\\ {p}^{2}-3p-10 & =0 \\\\ p & =\\frac{-(-3)\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-10)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=5.$"] [17592186287826 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0+9)-4(0-6)+1(0+2) \\\\  & =18+24+2 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{44}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287827 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287828 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =15-(-1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\cdot 16=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287829 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22}=\\frac{1}{-16}\\cdot 4=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287830 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-0+(0-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287831 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}=\\frac{1}{39}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{39}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287832 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =24-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 24=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287833 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-3-10) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{71}\\cdot 13=\\frac{13}{71}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287886 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186287887 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-k \\\\  & =2i+j \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(3i+j+k)-k \\\\  & =3i+j \\\\  & =⟨3,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186287896 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot 0)-0+(5\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-12-0-2 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287957 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =(0)i-(0)j+(8-18)k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287958 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((0)(1)-(0)(3))i-((0)(1)-(2)(3))j+((0)(0)-(2)(0))k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-6)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287959 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2x & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2x & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4-15)i-(2x-6x)j+(5x-4x)k \\\\  & =⟨-11,4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121} & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+121 & =393 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =272 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 4.$"] [17592186287960 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((5)(0)-(3)(4))i-((1)(0)-(2)(4))j+((1)(3)-(2)(5))k \\\\  & =(0-12)i-(0-8)j+(3-10)k \\\\  & =-12i+8j-7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287961 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)(-3)-(2)(0))i-((-2)(-3)-(0)(0))j+((-2)(2)-(0)(2))k \\\\  & =(-6-0)i-(6-0)j+(-4-0)k \\\\  & =-6i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287968 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))i-(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,u\\times v\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287969 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\times g & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-2))j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =5i-7j-k \\\\  & =⟨5,-7,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,f\\times g\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287970 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h\\times l & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-3i+6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,6,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,h\\times l\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287971 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(2i-2j+2k)-(3i-2j+k) \\\\  & =-i+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(i-3j+3k)-(3i-2j+k) \\\\  & =-2i-j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)i-((-1)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)j+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =i+k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186287972 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)-3(3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =4-3-16 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287973 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3)+2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-12-2+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287979 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+3j+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-j+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-4i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+4k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)-2((-4)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)+((-4)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-4+20+4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|20|=20.\n$$"] [17592186292813 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-((-4)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)j+((-4)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =4i-4j+8k \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292814 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-4)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =2i-4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [17592186292815 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+3j-4k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2j+2k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =3i-3j-4k \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186292816 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2j-k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-3i-2j)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-2i-2j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292832 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 0)i-(6\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 5)j+(6\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =(6-0)i-(-18-0)j+(0-(-10))k \\\\  & =6i+18j+10k \\\\  & =⟨6,18,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292833 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2x & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 5 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(-2x-10x)j+(0-6x)k \\\\  & =⟨3,12x,-6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(12x)^{2}+(-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+144{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ \\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ 180{x}^{2}+9 & =909 \\\\ 180{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186292840 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-4i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4+2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292841 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & k \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(k\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2)-((-1)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2)-3((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-8k-8+10-6+9k \\\\  & =k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ k-4 & =0 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=4.$"] [17592186292868 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =-5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5(-21-(-10))-3(-3-0)+0 \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-11)-3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =55+9 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292885 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2+2)-0-3(2-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(6-0) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292886 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-k)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292887 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2+k)-(-4+2)-(2k+2) \\\\  & =2+k+2-2k-2 \\\\  & =2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ -1\\cdot (k-2) & =2 \\\\ k-2 & =-2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295036 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-2))j+(5\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =(8+3)i-(20-6)j+(5+4)k \\\\  & =11\\,i-14\\,j+9\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295037 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(x-2)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =⟨2,2-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4-4x+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+24 & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=2.$"] [17592186295127 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i-2j+k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+4j-k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =-2i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+j)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(8-1)+2(-4-1)+3(-2-4) \\\\  & =14-10-18 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-14|=14.\n$$"] [17592186295128 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i-j+5k)-(i-3j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+k)-(i-3j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(3i-2j+3k)-(i-3j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1)-2((-3)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-3)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(3+1)-2(-3+2)+3(-3-6) \\\\  & =12+2-27 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-13|=13.\n$$"] [17592186295135 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-7\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-7(0-(-20))+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot (20)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-140-6 \\\\  & =-146\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295136 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(0-0)-3(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295137 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3)+2(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))+5(4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =3(2)+2(8)+5(12+1) \\\\  & =6+16+5(13) \\\\  & =6+16+65 \\\\  & =87\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295138 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))+2(4\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)+0 \\\\  & =1(6+5)+2(8-30) \\\\  & =11+2(-22) \\\\  & =11-44 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295139 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(4\\cdot 1-6\\cdot (-3))+2((-1)(1)-6\\cdot 2)+3((-1)(-3)-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =5(4+18)+2(-1-12)+3(3-8) \\\\  & =5(22)+2(-13)+3(-5) \\\\  & =110-26-15 \\\\  & =69\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295140 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(x\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)-(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(x-20)-(3+8) \\\\  & =2x-40-11 \\\\  & =2x-51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $37,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-51 & =37 \\\\ 2x & =88 \\\\ x & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295141 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =\\begin{vmatrix}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(k+1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2k\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(k+1)(5\\cdot 1-0\\cdot k)-2k(0\\cdot 1-0\\cdot -2)+3(0\\cdot k-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5)-2k(0)+3(10) \\\\  & =5k+5+30 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =-35 \\\\ k & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295151 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ x+1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-(2x-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+6\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & x+1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(3\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (x+1))-(2x-1)(0)+6(0\\cdot (x+1)-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =x(-12)+0+6(3) \\\\  & =-12x+18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+18 & =0 \\\\ -12x & =-18 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295160 :question/worked-solution "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}x & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(-2\\cdot 0-(-1)(4))-3(x\\cdot 0-(-1)(1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(4)-3(1) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-8-3 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $32$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-11 & =32 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =43 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{43}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{43}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}.$"] [17592186295161 :question/worked-solution "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 7)+5(3-7x) \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(-22)+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+44x+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+5x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $40$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x+15 & =40 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-25 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4(2)(-25)}}{2(2)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5+\\sqrt{225}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-5$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [17592186295164 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1-4-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"] [17592186295165 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))-1((-3)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 4)+2((-3)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2+3-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|1|=1.\n$$"] [17592186295166 :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0)+3(-20) \\\\  & =30-0-60 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-30|=30.\n$$"]}
